1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,917
Two versions of you. Same job. Same salary. Same two hundred dollars a month.

2
00:00:05,237 --> 00:00:09,022
One starts investing at eighteen. The other waits until thirty.

3
00:00:09,342 --> 00:00:11,513
Twelve years. That's the only difference.

4
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:15,781
By the end of this video, one of them has almost two hundred thousand dollars more than the other.

5
00:00:16,101 --> 00:00:21,023
And the twist is, for the first twelve years, the early starter looks like a complete idiot.

6
00:00:21,823 --> 00:00:24,331
It's January nineteen ninety eight. You're eighteen.

7
00:00:24,651 --> 00:00:29,179
You have a part time job, a terrible haircut, and a grandma with opinions.

8
00:00:29,499 --> 00:00:33,504
Sweetheart, put a little away every month. Don't touch it. Just let it grow.

9
00:00:33,824 --> 00:00:37,075
Two hundred dollars a month. That's a lot of pizza at eighteen.

10
00:00:37,395 --> 00:00:39,160
And here the timeline splits.

11
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:48,656
In timeline A, you listen to grandma. Two hundred dollars a month into an S and P five hundred index fund, starting right now, at eighteen.

12
00:00:48,976 --> 00:00:55,663
In timeline B, you think, I'm young. I'll start later. And you do. At thirty, in January twenty ten.

13
00:00:55,983 --> 00:01:01,324
Same fund. Same two hundred a month. Both keep going until September twenty twenty six.

14
00:01:01,644 --> 00:01:05,928
We'll use real historical prices. Every crash included.

15
00:01:06,728 --> 00:01:09,421
Timeline A. The first two years feel amazing.

16
00:01:09,741 --> 00:01:15,256
It's the late nineties. Everyone is talking about the internet. Your neighbour quit his job to trade stocks.

17
00:01:15,576 --> 00:01:22,066
By August two thousand, you've put in six thousand four hundred dollars, and it's worth over seven thousand six hundred.

18
00:01:22,386 --> 00:01:25,602
You feel like a genius. You tell everyone at parties.

19
00:01:26,402 --> 00:01:28,236
Then the internet bubble pops.

20
00:01:28,556 --> 00:01:32,898
By October two thousand two, you've put in eleven thousand six hundred dollars.

21
00:01:33,218 --> 00:01:35,343
And it's worth eight thousand three hundred.

22
00:01:35,663 --> 00:01:38,078
You've lost money. Real money. Pizza money.

23
00:01:38,398 --> 00:01:41,916
Every month, you're buying more of something that keeps going down.

24
00:01:42,716 --> 00:01:46,454
Meanwhile, in timeline B, you're having the time of your life.

25
00:01:46,774 --> 00:01:53,473
No investing. Just road trips with Max. Concert tickets. A used car with a sound system louder than its engine.

26
00:01:53,793 --> 00:01:57,787
Max raises a glass. To never checking a stock price, ever.

27
00:01:58,107 --> 00:02:00,754
Honestly? Timeline B looks smarter right now.

28
00:02:01,554 --> 00:02:06,918
Back in timeline A, things get worse. Two thousand eight. The global financial crisis.

29
00:02:07,238 --> 00:02:10,303
Banks collapse on the news. People lose their homes.

30
00:02:10,623 --> 00:02:15,882
By March two thousand nine, you've put in twenty seven thousand dollars over eleven years.

31
00:02:16,202 --> 00:02:18,687
And it's worth seventeen thousand four hundred.

32
00:02:19,007 --> 00:02:23,012
Eleven years of discipline, and you're almost ten thousand dollars down.

33
00:02:23,332 --> 00:02:26,374
Every voice in your head says sell. Stop. You were wrong.

34
00:02:27,174 --> 00:02:29,902
And here, the timeline could split one more time.

35
00:02:30,222 --> 00:02:34,390
Imagine a version of you who can't take it anymore. In March two thousand nine, at the very bottom, you sell everything.

36
00:02:34,710 --> 00:02:37,404
Seventeen thousand four hundred dollars. You put it in a savings account, swear you'll never touch stocks again, and you keep that promise.

37
00:02:37,724 --> 00:02:39,953
That version of you locks in the loss forever. Hold on to that number. Seventeen thousand four hundred. We'll come back to it.

38
00:02:40,273 --> 00:02:46,356
But in our timeline A, you do the hardest thing in investing. Absolutely nothing. You keep buying.

39
00:02:47,156 --> 00:02:53,530
Then you turn thirty. December two thousand nine.

40
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:57,275
Twelve years of investing. Twenty eight thousand eight hundred dollars put in.

41
00:02:57,595 --> 00:03:03,284
Your account says twenty seven thousand six hundred.

42
00:03:03,604 --> 00:03:07,888
Less than you put in. After twelve years.

43
00:03:08,208 --> 00:03:14,443
Timeline B has zero dollars invested, but a lot of good memories. Max is laughing at you. Openly.

44
00:03:14,763 --> 00:03:17,445
This is the exact moment most people quit forever.

45
00:03:18,245 --> 00:03:22,482
And now timeline B wakes up. You're thirty. You've got a better job. You read one article about compound interest.

46
00:03:22,802 --> 00:03:24,079
January twenty ten. You start the same two hundred dollars a month.

47
00:03:24,399 --> 00:03:30,460
And here's the thing. Your timing is incredible. You start right after the crash, when prices are low.

48
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:33,357
Timeline A has to keep going. Timeline B gets a fresh start at a discount.

49
00:03:34,157 --> 00:03:36,920
So now both timelines invest the same two hundred dollars every month.

50
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:43,823
Watch the two lines.

51
00:03:44,143 --> 00:03:45,072
Timeline A has twelve extra years of shares. And now, finally, those twelve years start to pay.

52
00:03:45,392 --> 00:03:49,804
Every rise in the market lifts a much bigger pile.

53
00:03:50,124 --> 00:03:53,201
Twenty twenty. Twenty twenty two. Both drop. Both keep buying. And the gap keeps getting wider.

54
00:03:54,001 --> 00:03:55,858
September twenty twenty six. Final numbers.

55
00:03:56,178 --> 00:04:03,423
Timeline B, starting at thirty: forty thousand two hundred dollars put in. Worth about one hundred and thirty thousand.

56
00:04:03,743 --> 00:04:07,911
Not bad at all.

57
00:04:08,231 --> 00:04:12,538
Timeline A, starting at eighteen: sixty nine thousand put in.

58
00:04:12,858 --> 00:04:18,059
Worth about three hundred and twenty one thousand dollars.

59
00:04:18,379 --> 00:04:28,294
A difference of about one hundred and ninety thousand dollars. From starting twelve years earlier.

60
00:04:29,094 --> 00:04:31,230
Max hears these numbers at a barbecue in twenty twenty six.

61
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:41,674
Wait. You're telling me the boring one is the rich one?

62
00:04:41,994 --> 00:04:46,905
Yes, Max. And remember the version who sold everything in two thousand nine? Still seventeen thousand four hundred dollars. Plus a little bank interest.

63
00:04:47,225 --> 00:04:48,514
Timeline A, who did nothing, has over eighteen times more.

64
00:04:48,834 --> 00:04:51,992
Max quietly opens an investing app under the table.

65
00:04:52,792 --> 00:04:56,786
But here's the craziest part.

66
00:04:57,106 --> 00:05:02,841
Imagine a third timeline. You start at eighteen, and at thirty, you stop. Completely. You never add another dollar.

67
00:05:03,161 --> 00:05:09,198
You put in twenty eight thousand eight hundred dollars. Less than timeline B.

68
00:05:09,518 --> 00:05:13,779
And by twenty twenty six, it's worth about one hundred and ninety thousand.

69
00:05:14,099 --> 00:05:19,301
The person who stopped at thirty beats the person who started at thirty. By sixty thousand dollars.

70
00:05:19,621 --> 00:05:29,570
And look closely at that number. It's exactly the gap between timelines A and B. That's not a coincidence. That gap is simply what those first twelve years grew into.

71
00:05:30,370 --> 00:05:36,500
So what would timeline B need to catch up?

72
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:41,290
Starting at thirty, you'd need to invest about four hundred and ninety two dollars every month, not two hundred, to end up with the same three hundred and twenty one thousand.

73
00:05:41,610 --> 00:05:47,682
Almost two and a half times more money, every month, for sixteen years.

74
00:05:48,002 --> 00:05:56,419
That's the price of waiting.

75
00:05:57,219 --> 00:05:59,646
Why does this happen? Because compounding loves time more than it loves money.

76
00:05:59,966 --> 00:06:04,447
The money from your first years has the longest hill to roll down. It grows, and then its growth grows.

77
00:06:04,767 --> 00:06:11,072
Those first twelve years looked like a total waste. Two crashes. No profit. But they were planting seeds.

78
00:06:11,392 --> 00:06:16,001
The hard part was never the math. It was staying calm while it looked like you were losing.

79
00:06:16,801 --> 00:06:19,692
Now, maybe you're not eighteen. Maybe you're thirty, or forty, or fifty. So is this video useless?

80
00:06:20,012 --> 00:06:25,898
No. Because there's always a version of you who is ten years older and wishes you'd started today.

81
00:06:26,218 --> 00:06:32,394
Timeline B still turned forty thousand dollars into about one hundred and thirty thousand. That's real money.

82
00:06:32,714 --> 00:06:41,445
And this works far beyond America. In Europe and most of the world, you can buy broad, low cost index funds that track thousands of companies.

83
00:06:41,765 --> 00:06:45,062
Even small amounts count. Twenty five dollars a month that actually happens beats five hundred dollars a month that you keep postponing.

84
00:06:45,382 --> 00:06:48,587
The best day to start was years ago. The second best is today.

85
00:06:49,387 --> 00:06:52,800
If you want to start, three boring rules.

86
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:58,008
One. Automate it. The money leaves your account the day you get paid, so you never have to decide.

87
00:06:58,328 --> 00:07:00,081
Two. Keep it cheap and broad. A low fee index fund beats picking the next hot stock for most people.

88
00:07:00,401 --> 00:07:04,952
Three. When the market crashes, don't look away from the plan. Look away from the news.

89
00:07:05,272 --> 00:07:12,621
And a bonus rule. Every time your income goes up, raise the monthly amount a little, before your lifestyle eats the raise.

90
00:07:12,941 --> 00:07:17,632
That's it. No secret stock. No guru. Just time, and a plan you don't break.

91
00:07:18,432 --> 00:07:21,276
And the honest part. This is not financial advice.

92
00:07:21,596 --> 00:07:27,645
These are real past prices, and the past doesn't promise the future. Some decades are much worse than others.

93
00:07:27,965 --> 00:07:34,315
Have an emergency fund, deal with expensive debt first, and only invest money you won't need for years.

94
00:07:35,115 --> 00:07:38,482
Twelve years. Two crashes. One grandma who was right.

95
00:07:38,802 --> 00:07:43,400
So tell me in the comments. How old were you when you started investing, or are you starting today?

96
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:45,496
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

